Experiment No. 5 – Password Testing Using Hydra

Date: 24/09/2026

Aim: To perform brute-force and dictionary attacks using Hydra on a test system to understand password vulnerabilities.

Tools Required:

Kali Linux, Hydra, authorized test server, test username and password wordlist.

Theory:

Hydra is a password-testing tool used to identify weak passwords. A dictionary attack tests passwords from a predefined wordlist, while a brute-force attack tries different character combinations.

Procedure:

Install and start Kali Linux.
Verify that Hydra is installed.
Set up an authorized test server and test account.
Prepare a password wordlist.
Perform dictionary and brute-force tests using Hydra in the lab environment.
Observe authentication attempts and record the results.
Monitor suspicious login attempts.

Types of Attacks:

Dictionary Attack: Tests passwords from a predefined wordlist.
Brute-Force Attack: Tries different password combinations until a match is found.

Prevention:

Use strong and unique passwords.
Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
Apply login attempt limits and account lockout.
Monitor suspicious login attempts.

Result:

Dictionary and brute-force password-testing techniques using Hydra were studied in an authorized test environment, and password security measures were understood.
